    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Ants live in colonies that operate without central control. Interactions among individual ants determine how a colony regulates its behavior to conserve water and energy (Greene, et al, 2013). Harvester ants interact in an entrance chamber just inside the nest entrance, as they come in and out of the nest. Whether an outgoing forager leaves the nest depends on its rate of interactions with returning foragers. My work investigates how, during a period of foraging, the number of ants in the entrance chamber and the locations of their interactions change over time. My project examines two aspects of the behavior of the red harvester ant (Pogonomyrmex barbatus): (1) how the rate of returning foragers affects the pool of outgoing foragers by regulating ants moving up to the entrance chamber from the deeper nest, and (2) how the shape of a colony’s entrance chamber determines the density and location of interactions. We created 16 films of 6 different ant colonies and recorded information about the number of ants moving in and out of the entrance chamber from the deeper nest, and also marked where ants were having interactions in the entrance chamber. \r\n\r\nWe found that the rate of returning foragers does influence the size of the pool of outgoing foragers, because the rate of returning foragers influences the movement of ants in and out of the entrance chamber from the deeper nest. We found a lag of 18 seconds for an ant to descend into the deeper nest after an ant has returned to the entrance chamber from an outside foraging trip. We also found that the descent of an ant into the deeper nest is also associated with an ant coming up from the deeper nest after a mean lag of 38.25 seconds. We also find that ants tend to have most of their interactions around the tunnel entrance and exit of the entrance chamber just inside the nest entrance. These relationships suggest that on a scale of seconds, returning foragers are regulating movement among chambers within a nest and thus changing the outgoing forager numbers. Ants tend to engage in interactions around entrances/exits. But interaction “hotspots” also vary among colonies, as colony nest shape varies.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
